/** @mainpage %Qore openldap Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section openldapintro Introduction

    The openldap module exposes functionality from the
    <a href="http://www.openldap.org">OpenLDAP</a> library as a %Qore API,
    allowing %Qore programs to communicate with LDAP servers.

    This module is released under the
    <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    and is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded
    unconditionally regardless of how the %Qore library was initialized).

    Like all %Qore components, the openldap module is thread-safe. The
    @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient "LdapClient" class represents a single network
    connection to the LDAP server and wraps every request in a
    mutual-exclusion lock to ensure atomicity and thread-safety.

    Asynchronous APIs are used internally to enforce time limits on each
    LDAP operation. The default timeout for all LDAP operations is set in
    the @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::constructor() "LdapClient::constructor()"
    method via the \c "timeout" option; each method requiring server
    communication also accepts an optional per-call timeout. If no
    \c "timeout" option is supplied to the constructor, the default is
    60 seconds.

    Key capabilities:
    - full typed client API through
      @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient "OpenLdap::LdapClient" — search, add,
      modify, delete, compare, rename, password change, extended operations
    - SASL bind with any mechanism (GSSAPI, EXTERNAL, SCRAM, DIGEST-MD5, …)
      and GSSAPI hardening controls including TLS channel binding — see
      @ref openldapsaslguide
    - paged results (RFC 2696) via the \c "page-size" search option; handles
      Active Directory's 1000-entry limit transparently
    - server and client controls on all LDAP operations; see
      @ref ldap_control_constants
    - binary attribute support with a built-in known-binary list and
      opt-in @c "binary-attributes" / @c "all-binary" options
    - LDAP transactions (RFC 5805) with RAII helper class
    - user modules for connection pooling, async operations, LDIF
      import/export, and Active Directory compatibility — see
      @ref openldaphelpermodules
    - sandboxed and interruptible I/O for graceful termination of
      long-running operations

    <b>Overview of Operations Supported by the LdapClient Class</b>
    |!Operation|!Method|!Description
    |search|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::search() "LdapClient::search()"|Search for entries and attributes; supports paged results via \c "page-size" option
    |add|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::add() "LdapClient::add()"|Add entries to the Directory Information Tree
    |modify|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::modify() "LdapClient::modify()"|Modify existing entries
    |delete|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::del() "LdapClient::del()"|Delete existing entries
    |compare|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::compare() "LdapClient::compare()"|Compare attribute values
    |rename|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::rename() "LdapClient::rename()"|Rename or move entries to another location in the Directory Information Tree
    |change password|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::passwd() "LdapClient::passwd()"|Change the LDAP password for a given user
    |SASL bind|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::saslBind() "LdapClient::saslBind()"|SASL bind with any mechanism (GSSAPI, DIGEST-MD5, EXTERNAL, etc.)
    |extended operation|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::extendedOp() "LdapClient::extendedOp()"|Perform generic LDAP extended operations (Who Am I?, Cancel, etc.)

    The underlying %LDAP functionality is provided by the
    <a href="http://www.openldap.org">OpenLDAP library</a>.

